=base:summary.cmark
A template can now compute with numbers, and will soon declare a whole structure of solver variables in one form. Both designs are written down and accepted; neither is in a release yet.

=base:main-content.cmark

# Two designs accepted: arithmetic, and shapes of symbols

Two language designs were accepted this week. This page says what each one
is and how far along it is. The short version:

<table>
<tr><th>design</th><th>accepted</th><th>specified</th><th>implemented</th></tr>
<tr><td>arithmetic, and integers and floats as two types</td><td>2026-10-06</td><td>yes, in the reference</td><td>PureScript (with the command-line tool, the Halogen fold and the playground) and Haskell, on <code>main</code>, not released</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>?shape</code>, a form that allocates a structure of symbols</td><td>2026-10-08</td><td>design only</td><td>no</td></tr>
</table>


Both are recorded in
[`specs/decisions.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/decisions.md),
sections 18 and 19, with the alternatives that were rejected and why.

## Arithmetic

Until now the reference said a template "compares and selects, it does not
compute". Every derived number was the host's job. That works when the host
and the template author are the same party. It fails when a template is
handed counters by a host it does not control: it cannot show a subtotal or a
share in percent unless someone upstream precomputes each one and stores it
next to the raw numbers.

So the position is reversed, narrowly. The grammar does not change. There are
no infix operators. Arithmetic is nine named builtins:

<table>
<tr><th>builtin</th><th>operands</th><th>result</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>sum(…)</code>, <code>product(…)</code></td><td>all integers or all floats</td><td>the sum, the product</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>negate(x)</code></td><td>an integer or a float</td><td><code>-x</code>, of the same type</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>quotient(a, b)</code>, <code>inverse(x)</code></td><td>floats</td><td>float division</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>floor-quotient(a, b)</code>, <code>modulo(a, b)</code></td><td>integers</td><td>integer division and its remainder</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>floor(x)</code></td><td>either</td><td>the largest integer not above <code>x</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>real(x)</code></td><td>either</td><td>the float nearest to <code>x</code></td></tr>
</table>


```
@subtotal = sum($ctx.compute, $ctx.storage, negate($ctx.credit))
@share    = branch(0, gt($ctx.total, 0),
                   floor-quotient(product(100, $ctx.used), $ctx.total))
@total    = sum(0.0, map($ctx.lines,
                         (l) => product(real($l.qty), real($l.price))))
```

### Integers and floats are two types

A counter must add exactly and a ratio must not, so the single number type
splits in two. A literal with neither a fraction nor an exponent is an
integer (`1`, `-7`); one with either is a float (`1.0`, `1e5`). A JSON number
in the context is read by the same rule.

Nothing converts between the two unless the program says so. `sum(1, 2.5)` is
an error, `eq(1, 1.0)` is `false`, and `gt(1.5, 0)` is written
`gt(1.5, 0.0)`. `real` and `floor` are the two conversions, and each accepts
both types, so a template that does not know which one its host sends
normalizes with `real($x)`.

**This changes existing output in one case.** A float keeps its type when it
is written, so a whole-valued float renders with a fraction: `str(1.0)` is
`1.0` where it was `1`. An integer renders as before.

### Over a symbol, arithmetic builds a term

In symbolic mode a number may not be known yet. `sum(1, $s, 2)` with a
symbol `$s` does not fail and does not guess. It evaluates to a *term*, the
computation handed to the host undone:

```json
{"$term": "sum", "arguments": [1, {"$sym": "#0:\"s\"", "path": []}, 2]}
```

The operands are kept exactly as written, with nothing folded. That buys one
law: a host that substitutes numbers for the symbols and evaluates the term
by the reference's rules gets what the template would have produced had
those numbers been in the context. Tramaj still solves nothing.

### Where it stands

The normative text is in the
[language reference](/reference-language.html) and the
[symbols reference](/reference-v3-symbols.html), and the conformance corpus
has the fixtures. The PureScript, Haskell and Rust implementations have the number
split and the nine builtins on `main`, and so do the command-line tool
(`tramaj-cli --arithmetic`), the Halogen fold and the
[playground](/playground.html). The Go implementation has both on `main`
too. They are not in a release. The Rust,
JavaScript and Python implementations follow.

The split changes what some existing templates produce, beyond the `str`
case above: see
[Migrating to integers and floats](/migrating-to-integers-and-floats.html).

## `?shape`: a structure of symbols in one form

In symbolic mode a template allocates a solver variable with `?(key)`. One
variable at a time is fine. A structure of them is not: every variable is
its own form, with a key composed by hand.

```
@cluster = {
  lb:         ?(["cluster", "lb"]),
  placements: map($ctx.vms, (vm) => {
    vm:   $vm.name,
    host: ?(["cluster", "host", $vm.name]),
    zone: ?(["cluster", "zone", $vm.name])
  })
}
```

The accepted design adds a second allocation form. `?shape(key, shape)`
takes the key once, and the shape says where the symbols go with a named
marker, `~name`:

```
@cluster = ?shape("cluster", {
  ~lb,
  placements: map($ctx.vms, (vm) => {vm: $vm.name, ~host($vm.name), ~zone($vm.name)})
})
```

This is "traverse" in the functional-programming sense: walk a structure, run
an effect at each position, get the same structure back. The effect is
allocation. The number of placements is whatever the caller's `vms` holds.

The rules are few:

- **A marker is explicit and named.** `~lb` is a symbol; nothing else in the
  shape is. `{~lb}` is short for `{lb: ~lb}`.
- **A marker may be written anywhere in the shape**: a field, an array
  element, a call's argument, the body of a `map`.
- **Under a lambda a marker carries its own key**, `~host($vm.name)`, which
  says which iteration it belongs to. A bare marker there is a parse error,
  so one variable is never shared across iterations by accident.
- **Identity is what the author wrote.** The symbols above are
  `#0:["cluster","lb"]`, `#0:["cluster","host","a"]` and so on: the form's
  key, the marker's name and the marker's key. No position and no index is
  involved, so reordering the `vms` keeps every variable's identity.
- **Mistakes are parse errors**: the same marker name twice in one form, or a
  shape with no marker at all.

It is a pure rewriting onto `?(key)`. The form above means exactly what the
hand-written version means, so the core language, the output envelope and
every host decoder stay as they are.

### Where it stands

This one is a design only. The normative text is not written and no
implementation has it. PureScript and Haskell come first, as for arithmetic.
One thing it does not cover is a caller that supplies a *count* rather than
a list: that needs a `range`, which is a separate question.

## Also accepted: sorting and number formatting

A third design was accepted on the same day, in section 20 of the same file:
`sort-by(list, fn)` and `sort-by-descending(list, fn)`, which take a key
function as `map` does, and `format-number(x, decimals, group)`, which turns
`1234567.891` into `1,234,567.89` by one rounding rule every implementation
must agree on, with `round` as a tenth arithmetic builtin. Its normative text
is now in the [language reference](/reference-language.html), marked
**[§20]**; no implementation has it yet.

